      "writtenOn": "2019-03-22T20:10:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Why go through all this trouble ?\n\nYou need a method for each resource in this class anyway. Why not have the Class as a constructor arg and read the field through reflection right here and do away with the proxy class and the obscure runtime annotation ?\n\npublic class ResourceManager {\n  private final Class\u003c?\u003e mR;\n  private final Resources mResources;\n\n  public ResourceManager(@NonNull Class\u003c?\u003e r, @NonNull Resources resources) {\n    mR \u003d r;\n    mResources \u003d resources;\n  }\n\n  private String fetchStringResource(@NonNull String path) {\n    // fetch subclass of R through reflection¹²³\n    // fetch the field from the subclass and the name\n    // fetch the value of the field\n    // lookup in mResources and return\n  }\n\n  public String getCaptivePortalHttpUrl() {\n    return readStringResource(\"config_captive_portal_http_url\");\n  }\n}\n\nThis sounds to me a lot simpler than what you have, and under better control. Few developers understand dynamic proxies too, or have a good handle on the difference between the annotation retentions.\n\n\nNotes :\n¹ Optionally here you may elect to have the path be something like \"string.config_captive_portal_http_url\" and automatically split and fetch the subclass.\n² You could also elect to fetch the list of subclasses immediately in the constructor and store them in members, or in a Map\u003cString, Class\u003c?\u003e\u003e, so that you don\u0027t have to look them up through reflection with every call\n³ You can even cache the fetched value here in a map, though I don\u0027t think it\u0027s worth it",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 59,
        "startChar": 34,
        "endLine": 59,
        "endChar": 56
      },
